  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1204 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nA large upper level high pressure area covering the southwest, \ncentral Rockies, and central and southern plains is currently \ncentered over southwestern Colorado. This ridge will slowly drift \nto the southwest as we move through the workweek, but remain in \ncontrol of our weather here through at least Friday. Early in the \nweek, highs will be in the mid 90s to around 100 degrees, but \ntemperatures will gradually warm throughout the week. By Friday, \nhighs will likely range from 98 to as hot as 107 degrees, with \nthe hottest temperatures in the Big Country. The forecast will \nalso remain dry through the workweek. Over the weekend, medium \nrange models suggest that a trough digging south into the \nsouthern Mississippi River valley, may weaken the ridge in our \narea. If that happens, we could see slightly cooler temperatures \nand for now, a low (20%) chance for showers and thunderstorms next\nweekend.",
